NAME
    keycloak - existing Keycloak server command plus direct HTTPS administration

SYNOPSIS
    keycloak admin [--server HTTPS-URL] [--realm ISSUER-REALM]
             [--client-id ID --client-secret-file LOCAL-FILE |
              --username USER --password-file LOCAL-FILE [--client-id ID] |
              --token-file LOCAL-FILE]
             [--ca-file LOCAL-PEM] discovery
    keycloak admin [same options] get|post|put|delete /admin/RESOURCE
             [--data-file JSON-FILE]

DESCRIPTION
    Without the 'admin' subcommand, keycloak delegates all operands unchanged
    to the existing Keycloak package command under bin/ext/keycloak. This
    preserves commands such as keycloak start-dev, build and show-config,
    including the package launcher's environment/state behavior. If the server
    package is not installed, non-admin forms return status 9.

    keycloak is an independent POSIX shell command in the technical m runtime.
    It calls Keycloak directly using curl, without rsudo, SSH, sudo, Chromium
    or a remote m installation. From an uninitialized shell use
    m keycloak admin [options] ....

    Each authenticated invocation obtains a fresh token and performs exactly
    one Admin REST operation. Three explicit credential sources are supported:
    (1) service-account client_credentials with a configured confidential
    client, (2) user password Direct Access Grant with a protected password
    file and selected client (default admin-cli), or (3) a pre-existing
    bearer token file. No tokens are persisted by the command or printed
    as authentication output. There is no implicit grant fallback.

    The user password grant is a compatibility path only when the selected
    Keycloak client permits Direct Access Grants. It cannot implement MFA,
    WebAuthn, conditional browser login or an external identity-provider
    redirect; in such environments use a valid existing bearer-token file
    or a specifically configured service account.

    The selected client must already exist and be configured with service
    accounts and the appropriate administrative roles. Authentication is
    not authorization: Keycloak can return HTTP 403 without those roles.

    discovery calls the public OIDC metadata endpoint without credentials.
    Request paths must start with /admin/. A path beginning with / is relative
    to the selected Keycloak base URL. Query strings are supported.

    GET/POST/PUT/DELETE are deliberately low-level: callers choose the exact
    Keycloak Admin REST path; no unknown resource schema or privileged default
    is inferred. POST and PUT may include JSON using --data-file.

OPTIONS
    --server HTTPS-URL
        HTTPS origin plus an optional reverse-proxy base path. Mandatory.
        HTTP, embedded credentials, query and fragment are rejected.
    --realm ISSUER-REALM
        Realm that issues the access token. Does not select the managed
        resource realm, which appears in the explicit /admin/ path.
    --client-id ID
        Explicit confidential service-account client, or selected Direct
        Access Grant client (default admin-cli only for username/password).
    --username USER
        Public username for an explicitly selected Direct Access Grant.
    --password-file LOCAL-FILE
        Protected local user password for the selected --username. Not
        combined with --client-secret-file or --token-file.
    --client-secret-file LOCAL-FILE
        Protected local one-line client secret. Never passed through curl
        ordinary argument values. No username/password grant is attempted.
    --token-file LOCAL-FILE
        Alternative protected local one-line bearer token. Cannot coexist
        with client-id or client-secret-file. Not silently refreshed.
    --ca-file LOCAL-PEM
        Optional approved PEM CA anchor for HTTPS verification.
    --data-file JSON-FILE
        Optional POST/PUT JSON request body; data remains file-backed.
    --help
        Print usage.

CREDENTIALS AND SECURITY
    The client secret, user password or bearer-token file must be a regular file, not a
    symlink, owned by the invoking user, readable and mode 0400, 0500,
    0600 or 0700. File content must contain one nonempty line, with at most
    one terminating newline. Secrets, tokens and authorization headers are
    staged in a private temporary directory with umask 077 and removed
    at completion. The curl configuration file is ignored (-q).

    HTTPS peer and CA verification remain enabled. The command does not
    follow redirects or accept non-HTTPS URLs. It does not enable a browser
    login or manage refresh, logout, token revocation or MFA sessions.
    Admin REST responses may contain sensitive data; stdout is the explicit
    user-requested response stream. Errors omit token/secret and response body.

ENVIRONMENT
    KEYCLOAK_SERVER              Default for --server
    KEYCLOAK_REALM               Default for --realm
    KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_ID           Default for --client-id
    KEYCLOAK_USERNAME            Default for --username
    KEYCLOAK_PASSWORD_FILE       Default for --password-file
    KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE  Default for --client-secret-file
    KEYCLOAK_TOKEN_FILE          Default for --token-file
    KEYCLOAK_CA_FILE             Default for --ca-file

    Each explicit option overrides its environment default.

DEPENDENCIES
    POSIX sh, curl, jq, mktemp. Local stat supports Linux (-c) and
    macOS/BSD (-f) credential ownership/permission inspection.

EXIT STATUS
    0  Success (response body on stdout, empty for HTTP 204).
    1  m environment unavailable.
    2  Invalid invocation or JSON input.
    3  Missing tool dependency.
    4  Local credential, CA or private-file failure.
    5  OIDC discovery or token HTTPS transport failure.
    6  OIDC discovery/token endpoint refused or malformed token response.
    7  Admin REST transport/response reading failure.
    8  Admin REST returned non-2xx status.
    9  Existing Keycloak server package launcher unavailable.

EXAMPLES
    m keycloak admin --server https://sso.example.org --realm master discovery

    m keycloak admin --server https://sso.example.org --realm master \
      --username admin --password-file "$HOME/.config/keycloak/admin-password" \
      get /admin/realms/master

    m keycloak admin --server https://sso.example.org --realm master \
      --client-id ops --client-secret-file "$HOME/.config/keycloak/secret" \
      get /admin/realms/master/users?max=5

    m keycloak admin --server https://sso.example.org --realm master \
      --client-id ops --client-secret-file "$HOME/.config/keycloak/secret" \
      post /admin/realms/master/users --data-file ./new-user.json

